{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01YFT2jshmKeXuUqLkmvz1T5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a complete understanding of all 4 files. Let me implement the changes. I'll work on all files in parallel using agents for the complex ones, or I can just do the edits directly since I understand the code well.\n\nLet me plan the changes:\n\n1. **api/orders.js**: Add upsell metafields to GraphQL, parse them, add upsell/overdue logic, create awaitingUpsell category, modify response\n2. **api/cron.js**: Add upsell metafields to GraphQL, parse them, add overdue section to email, modify subject\n3. **api/send-email.js**: Add upsell metafields to GraphQL, parse them, add upsell/overdue columns to CSV, overdue section to email\n\n\n4. **src/App.jsx**: Add awaitingUpsell section, overdue badges, upsell status display\n\nLet me start editing each file. I need to clarify the fulfillment cutoff times—the task specifies 7:00 AM PHT and 12:00 PM PHT daily, but the existing code uses 7:30 AM and 12:00 PM for sexual health products, so I'll need to check if this is a general change or product-specific.\n\nLooking at the requirements more carefully, upsell orders have their own overdue calculation based on when upsell_paid was updated, using the 7:00 AM and 12:00 PM cutoffs. For regular orders without upsells, I should keep the existing behavior tied to approved_to_ship.updatedAt with the same cutoff times. So it seems like the 7:00 AM and 12:00 PM cutoffs apply across the board now, replacing the previous 7:30 AM cutoff.\n\nThe implementation approach is straightforward: upsell overdue is just checking whether any 7:00 AM or 12:00 PM cutoff has passed since the upsell_paid_at timestamp. I'll start making the changes file by file, beginning with adding the upsell metafields to the GraphQL schema in orders.js. Still writing the overdue logic... I'm realizing there's a simpler approach here—instead of iterating through every day, I can just find the next 7 AM or 12 PM PHT cutoff after the start time and check if the current time has passed it.\n\nWriting cutoff logic... Still writing the time conversion logic...\n\nNow I'm checking whether an order is overdue by determining its start time based on whether it's an upsell or regular order, then comparing it against the next available cutoff window. I think this approach is cleaner than my previous attempt. Let me map out the changes I need to make across the codebase, starting with adding the upsell fields to the GraphQL query and then updating the order processing logic. I need to finish parsing the upsell data in the App component, then add the new tab and visual indicators for awaiting upsell orders and overdue status. Rather than coordinating across multiple agents, I'll just make the edits directly since I have a clear picture of what needs to change. Starting with orders.js to update the GraphQL query with the upsell metafields...\n\nAdding GraphQL query fields... Continuing with the upsell_paid parsing logic, I'm extracting and normalizing that metafield value the same way, then capturing its timestamp only when the value is true. Finally, I'm adding all three fields—upsell, upsell_paid, and upsell_paid_at—to the returned object.\n\nNow I need to refactor the filtering logic to handle two separate order categories: one for orders that are approved and either don't need an upsell or have already paid for it, and another for orders stuck waiting on upsell payment despite being approved to ship.\n\nI'll also add an overdue helper function to flag orders that have exceeded their payment window, then apply these changes across the codebase—starting with the GraphQL queries to fetch the upsell metafields, then updating the order processing logic, and finally syncing the same changes through the cron job, email service, and frontend.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,